- `partitionTurn` walks the whole block list every render. Items are already
  keyed by identity (`itemRenderKey`), so an incremental walk could reuse
  unchanged items per frame instead of re-walking the turn. Not needed at
  current sizes; revisit if streaming with many long turns shows partition
  cost. (Noted during the #558 render-model work, 2026-09-18.)

- Performance, further (from the `chat-render-perf` investigation, 2026-09-18).
  #558 removed the structural costs — per-token full-document reparse,
  per-token store update/render, per-revision forced layout. These remain, all
  unquantified; measure with `react renders` plus a `trace` during a long
  stream before implementing:
  - **Tail-bound the streaming markdown repair.** While streaming, Streamdown
    runs incomplete-markdown repair (`remend`) and the block split over the
    entire document on every content change. The per-block `memo` keeps settled
    blocks from re-rendering, but that parent pass is still O(n) per token —
    the same shape as the reparse #558 removed, just cheaper. Repair and split
    only the unstable tail, copying settled blocks raw (assistant-ui's
    `tailBoundedRemend` is the reference).
  - **Dirty-track transcript row identity.** The store replaces the turns array
    per stream update, so `TurnList`/the virtualizer (and non-memoized
    neighbors like `ChatSurface`/`Composer`) do work per frame. Coalescing
    already cut this to one update per frame; a stable row id-array identity
    across content-only updates would let the virtualizer and memoized rows
    skip.
  - **Render-count attribution is still unmeasured.** No clean frame-level A/B
    was captured for #558; profile per-component render counts per frame to
    decide whether either item above is worth doing.

